<header class="main-header">
  <div class="series-title">Likutay Halachos · Choshen Mishpat</div>
  <h1>Hilchos Halva'ah 5</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">Laws of Lending — 5 (Osiyos 1–6)</div>
</header>
<div class="halachah-intro">
The matter of lending with a sh'tar: one must be careful to lend with a sh'tar specifically, for then [the borrower] cannot deny etc. — as our Sages, of blessed memory, warned about this greatly, as is explained in the Gemara. Based on the Torah [teaching of Rabbeinu]: <span class="scripture">"Who is the man who desires life"</span> etc.; see there.
</div>
<div class="verse-block">
  <span class="scripture">Yamim tovim and yamim ra'im — finding good within the bad — the root of all debt and its rectification</span>
  <span class="source-ref">Based on Likutay Moharan I:54 "Mi HaIsh HeChafetz Chaim"</span>
</div>
<div class="os-header">Os 1</div>
<p>For the <span class="key-term">halva'ah</span> [lending/borrowing] is drawn through the fact that there are <span class="key-term">y'mai tov</span> [good days] and <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> [evil days]. For the loan comes about due to the lack of livelihood — that one does not have [what he needs] now, and he anticipates that after some days he will be able to repay him. And this is because he thinks that now the time is for him in the aspect of <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span>, from where all the lack of livelihood comes. And therefore he borrows from his fellow, that he should wait for him until the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> pass (as it is common in the mouths of the world to say each time that the times are bad now — <span class="aramaic">"di tzayt iz shlecht"</span> — and in truth all the days of their lives they say this; and from my youth until this day I recall that they say this each time, and so too in the previous generations they would say this each time). And then he will be able to pay him off.</p>
<p>And therefore each person must distance himself greatly from loans. For one must believe that even within the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> themselves there is good — the aspect of <span class="key-term">y'mai tov</span> — in the aspect of <span class="scripture">"and on a day of evil, see"</span> <span class="source">(Ecclesiastes 7:14)</span>: <span class="precise">"see"</span> specifically etc., as is written there. And then certainly he will be able to obtain his livelihood — the matter of each day in its day — and he will not need to come to loans and debts.</p>
<p>For even one who engages in commerce with the nations must know and believe that even there Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> is found — for in all languages etc. are clothed the letters of the Torah etc. And one who believes in this, through the power of the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> who conquers his <span class="key-term">yetzer</span> etc. — certainly his livelihood will not be lacking. For he will be able to find the letters of the Torah in all the languages etc., and build Torah from them, in the aspect of <span class="scripture">"those who do His word, to hearken to the voice of His word"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 103:20)</span> etc. And then certainly an abundance of goodness and blessing will be drawn to him through the Torah that he refines and builds from there — for the Torah is the source of blessing and abundance etc.</p>
<p>But through the overpowering of the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> over the <span class="key-term">y'mai tov</span>, until it becomes hidden from him that even there within the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> themselves good is found — through this he cannot find the letters of the Torah in his commerce and business. And then the abundance descends in great constriction, as is brought there — that this is the secret of <span class="aramaic">"ad d'lo yada bain arur Haman"</span> etc. [<span class="key-term">until one does not know between cursed Haman</span> etc.]. And through this he is compelled to come to loans — because he reckons that now are the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> and the <span class="key-term">y'mai tov</span> will come afterward, and he will pay him off.</p>
<p>And therefore, in truth, since he has come to loans it is very difficult for him to get out of them. And usually he does not pay off on time, as our Sages, of blessed memory, said: <span class="aramaic">"If only [halevai] he would repay on time."</span> <span class="precise">"Halevai"</span> indeed — for since he does not believe that even there, within the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> themselves, there is good, how will he attain <span class="key-term">y'mai tov</span>? For in truth, the <span class="key-term">y'mai tov</span> and <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> are present each and every day — only each person according to how he overcomes his <span class="key-term">yetzer</span>, to make the good prevail over the evil, so is the aspect of <span class="key-term">y'mai tov</span> drawn upon him, and the reverse to the reverse, <em>chas v'sholom</em>.</p>
<p>And the essential thing is that one must look carefully and believe that even within the evil itself — the aspect of the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> — even there good is found, for <span class="scripture">"the whole earth is full of His glory"</span> etc., as above. But this one who has fallen into loans — through the fact that he cannot find <span class="key-term">y'mai tov</span> within the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> — it is certainly difficult for him to emerge from debts. For the evil each time grows stronger, since he does not exert himself and look to find the good within the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> themselves — for through this is the essential overpowering of the evil.</p>
<p>And therefore, in truth, Rabbeinu, of blessed memory, warned that one should be careful not to become a debtor — and especially one who wishes to do <span class="key-term">t'shuvah</span>. As is written in Sefer HaAlef-Beis HaKadosh, and this is its language: "One who wishes to return should be careful not to become a debtor." For since he knows within himself that he has sinned and made the evil prevail over the good, and now he wishes to return — and the essential <span class="key-term">t'shuvah</span> is through finding the good within the evil itself, and believing that there is still good within him and he still has hope to return — in the aspect of <span class="scripture">"and you shall seek from there Hashem your G-d"</span> <span class="source">(Deuteronomy 4:29)</span>: <span class="precise">"from there"</span> specifically — from all the places where he was driven among the nations, as is understood there in the parashah. Therefore he certainly must be careful not to become a debtor — for [being a debtor] stems from not believing and not looking to see that even in the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> there is good, from where all the loans and debts are drawn, as above.</p>
<div class="os-header">Os 2</div>
<p>And therefore it is forbidden to lend without writing — that is, a <span class="key-term">sh'tar</span>. For writing is for the purpose of <span class="key-term">zikaron</span> [remembrance], as it is written: <span class="scripture">"Write this as a remembrance"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Exodus 17:14)</span>. And the essential nature of forgetfulness and remembrance is within time. For above time — in the aspect of the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'da'as</span> [love that is within the mind/knowledge] — there, forgetfulness and remembrance are not relevant, since it is above time. For forgetfulness is only with the passing of time, as it is written: <span class="scripture">"With the increase of days, everything is forgotten"</span> <span class="source">(Ecclesiastes 2:16)</span>.</p>
<p>And the essential forgetfulness that depends on time is due to the overpowering of the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> over the <span class="key-term">y'mai tov</span> — from where comes the forgetfulness that is drawn from the evil that takes hold from the departure of the <span class="key-term">mochin</span> [mental faculties], from where is the grip of forgetfulness. And therefore one needs writing as a remembrance.</p>
<p>It emerges that the essential purpose of writing — which is for remembrance — is to make the good prevail and to subdue the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span>. Therefore it is forbidden to lend without a <span class="key-term">sh'tar</span>. For the essential settling of debts is through making the good prevail over the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span>, which is accomplished through writing specifically — which is for remembrance, as above. For settling a debt is a <span class="key-term">mitzvah</span>, as our Sages, of blessed memory, said: <span class="aramaic">"The repayment of a creditor is a mitzvah"</span> <span class="source">(Kesubos 86a)</span>. For settling the debt is accomplished through meriting to attain the abundance and blessing to settle the debt — which comes from the prevailing of the good over the evil, which is drawn through the aspect of writing that is for remembrance, as above.</p>
<p>And therefore one who falls into loans falls into servitude, in the aspect of <span class="scripture">"the borrower is a servant to the lending man"</span> <span class="source">(Proverbs 22:7)</span>. For servitude is the aspect of <span class="scripture">"cursed is Canaan"</span> <span class="source">(Genesis 9:25)</span>, the aspect of the curse stated regarding the serpent, as is written elsewhere. This is the aspect of <span class="scripture">"cursed is the ground"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Genesis 3:17)</span>, the aspect of <span class="scripture">"by the sweat of your brow"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Genesis 3:19)</span> — from where all the toils and servitude of livelihood come, which are drawn from the sin of Adam HaRishon, who did not overcome [his <span class="key-term">yetzer</span>] to subdue the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> against the <span class="key-term">y'mai tov</span>, and ate from the <span class="key-term">Aitz HaDa'as Tov VaRa</span> [Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil]. Regarding which it is said: <span class="scripture">"A fool — on the day [bayom] his anger is known"</span> <span class="source">(Proverbs 12:16)</span>: <span class="precise">"on the day"</span> specifically — the aspect of <span class="scripture">"on the day you eat of it"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Genesis 2:17)</span>: <span class="precise">"on the day"</span> specifically. For the essential blemish reached into the <span class="key-term">yom</span> [day] — that he did not exert himself to find the good on that day, to make it prevail over the evil etc. And from there are drawn all the loans, which come from the lack of livelihood etc., as above.</p>
<p>And therefore the essential <span class="key-term">shibud</span> [lien/encumbrance] is through the <span class="key-term">sh'tar</span> — that then [the lender] can seize from encumbered properties. For the writing subdues the evil in favor of the good, until [the borrower] is compelled to pay him off and cannot deny him. For all the delay in payment and the denials are drawn from the overpowering of the evil afterward so greatly, until it prevents him from paying on time — in the aspect of <span class="scripture">"the wicked one borrows and does not repay"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 37:21)</span>. That the verse attributes all the wickedness of the wicked to the name "a wicked borrower" etc. — for the essential wickedness stems from the overpowering of the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> over the <span class="key-term">y'mai tov</span>, from where all the loans and the delay in payment come, as above.</p>
<p>And to subdue all of this — until [the borrower] is compelled to pay off and subdue the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> in favor of the good — is through writing as a remembrance, which is the <span class="key-term">sh'tar</span>, as above. And therefore the Torah warned: <span class="scripture">"If you lend money... to the poor... you shall not be to him as a creditor; you shall not place upon him usury"</span> <span class="source">(Exodus 22:24)</span> — for one must have mercy on the poor person, not to be upon him as a creditor, to bring him out of [the state of] being a debtor, as above.</p>
<div class="os-header">Os 3</div>
<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Rosh Hashanah</span> and <span class="key-term">Yom HaKippurim</span> and <span class="key-term">Sukkos</span> and <span class="key-term">Hoshana Rabbah</span> and <span class="key-term">Sh'mini Atzeres</span> and <span class="key-term">Simchas Torah</span>. For from Rosh Hashanah until Yom Kippur are the <span class="key-term">Aseres Y'mai T'shuvah</span> [Ten Days of Repentance], and the essential <span class="key-term">t'shuvah</span> is through the aforementioned aspect — the aspect of <span class="scripture">"and on a day of evil, see"</span> etc., as above.</p>
<p>And through the <span class="key-term">t'shuvah</span> of Rosh Hashanah and Yom HaKippurim — through finding the good in every place, through the <span class="key-term">kol shofar</span> — the aspect of <span class="scripture">"those who do His word, to hearken to the voice of His word"</span>, which is stated regarding Rosh Hashanah, as is written in the holy Zohar — and this is the aspect of the five afflictions [<span class="key-term">inuyim</span>] of Yom HaKippurim, which are the aspect of the five <span class="key-term">kolos</span> [voices]; and all of this is to unify the <span class="key-term">kol</span> with the <span class="key-term">dibur</span> [speech] etc. — through this they build the Torah. This is the aspect of the receiving of the Torah on Yom HaKippurim.</p>
<p>And through this they draw close to <span class="key-term">shalom</span> [peace], which is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Sukkah</span> — the aspect of <span class="key-term">Sukkas Shalom</span> [the Shelter of Peace] — the aspect of the <span class="key-term">ahavah</span> [love] that one merits through this. This is also the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Sukkah</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">ahavah</span> — until one merits through this the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'da'as</span> [love that is within the mind/knowledge]: to illuminate the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'da'as</span> into the days. This is the entire intention of the <span class="key-term">na'anu'im</span> [wavings] with the <span class="key-term">Arba'ah Minim</span> [Four Species] on Sukkos, as is explained at the end of the aforementioned Torah; see there.</p>
<p>And through this one merits the <span class="key-term">Simchas Beis HaSho'aivah</span>, in the aspect of <span class="scripture">"and you shall draw water with joy from the wellsprings of salvation"</span> <span class="source">(Isaiah 12:3)</span>. For there, in the aspect of the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'da'as</span>, are the <span class="key-term">ma'ayanai hayeshuah</span> [wellsprings of salvation]. For the Torah is called <span class="scripture">"a store of salvations"</span> <span class="source">(Isaiah 33:6)</span> etc. — but the hidden Torah etc. is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">ma'ayanai hayeshuah</span>.</p>
<p>And through this one merits <span class="key-term">Sh'mini Atzeres</span> — in the sense of <span class="key-term">k'nishah</span> [gathering] and assembly: that one gathers and encompasses the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'da'as</span> with the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'yamim</span> [love that is within the days] — where is the source of <span class="key-term">simchah</span>.</p>
<p>And this is the aspect of the joining of the completion of the Torah with its beginning. <span class="scripture">"Bereishis"</span> — the aspect of <span class="key-term">reishis hamachashavah</span> [the beginning of thought], the aspect of <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'da'as</span>, the aspect of <span class="aramaic">"Yisroel arose in [G-d's] thought first"</span> etc. [Connected to:] <span class="scripture">"for all the signs and wonders... before the eyes of all Yisroel"</span> <span class="source">(Deuteronomy 34:11–12)</span> — these are the aspect of the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'yamim</span>, from where all the revealed Torah comes, which is "before the eyes of all Yisroel": that He showed His love, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, before the eyes of all Yisroel through all the signs etc.</p>
<p>But the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'da'as</span> is impossible to see — except for each person according to what he estimates in his heart. Yet all the revelation of love through the awesome wonders that Moshe showed — all of it is drawn from the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'da'as</span> that illuminated into the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'yamim</span> — through his overturning the natural order within the days and time specifically. This is the essential completeness. Therefore each year, at the conclusion of the rectification of Rosh Hashanah, one must encompass the Torah's end within its beginning — to encompass the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'yamim</span> within the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'da'as</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="scripture">"Bereishis"</span> etc.</p>
<div class="os-header">Os 4</div>
<p>And this is why we bless for rain on Sh'mini Atzeres. For then the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'da'as</span> is encompassed and shines into the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'yamim</span>. And therefore then is the essential love between Yisroel and their Father in Heaven — as our Sages, of blessed memory, elaborated regarding this: as it is written, <span class="aramaic">"I beseech you, stay with Me one more day"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Sukkah 55b)</span>. The aspect of <span class="scripture">"one day"</span> — <span class="scripture">"it shall be known to Hashem"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Zechariah 14:7)</span> — which is the aspect of the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'da'as</span> etc.</p>
<p>And therefore then we bless for rain — for then the <span class="key-term">shefa</span> [abundance] is drawn, which is the aspect of the waters of rain and blessing, in the aspect of <span class="scripture">"Hashem will open for you His good treasure"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Deuteronomy 28:12)</span>. For in the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'yamim</span> — the aspect of <span class="scripture">"by day Hashem commands His lovingkindness"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 42:9)</span> — which are the aspect of the seven days of Sukkos: in them there is still some grip for the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span>, from which is the grip of the seventy nations. Only that on Sukkos, Yisroel merit — through the service of Rosh Hashanah and Yom HaKippurim, through the power of the true <span class="key-term">Tzadikim</span> — to draw close to the love and peace that are the aspect of the <span class="key-term">Sukkah</span>, as above. For we have already merited to find the good — which are the letters of the Torah — within the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> themselves, that is, the good within the seventy languages of the nations.</p>
<p>And through this — that we draw close to <span class="key-term">shalom</span>, having great peace with the nations since we have merited to find even among them Torah, as above — this is the aspect of the seventy <span class="key-term">parai hechag</span> [bulls of the festival] etc. And then, on Sukkos, through the <span class="key-term">Arba'ah Minim</span> we work through the <span class="key-term">na'anu'im</span> to draw an illumination from the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'da'as</span> into the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'yamim</span>.</p>
<p>And this is the aspect of the binding of the <span class="key-term">Arvai Nachal</span> [willow branches] with the <span class="key-term">Arba'ah Minim</span>. For the <span class="key-term">Arvai Nachal</span> allude to the transgressors of Yisroel, as our Sages, of blessed memory, said <span class="source">(Vayikra Rabbah 30:12)</span>. For now we are finding also the good in all the transgressors of Yisroel — in the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"If a person says to you, 'Where is your G-d?'"</span> etc. — as is written there in the aforementioned Torah.</p>
<p>And on <span class="key-term">Hoshana Rabbah</span>, at the conclusion of the sealing, we perform great services with the <span class="key-term">Arvai Nachal</span> themselves, and we beat them on the ground — to subdue and nullify from them the <span class="key-term">dinim</span> and the concealments and the hiddennesses, to demonstrate that even within the <span class="key-term">Arvai Nachal</span> themselves one finds Torah and service, even when they are on their own. For the Torah that is within the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> has been revealed etc.</p>
<p>And through all of this, one merits on Sh'mini Atzeres that all these rectifications are completed in wondrous perfection — for the essential completeness is when the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'da'as</span> fully illuminates into the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'yamim</span>. This is the aspect of Sh'mini Atzeres. And then the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> are entirely nullified, and therefore there is no grip for the nations, as our Sages, of blessed memory, said — only Yisroel themselves unify and connect with Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> in surpassing love, through the illumination that shines upon them then of the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'da'as</span>, with wondrous radiance.</p>
<p>And therefore then, specifically, they draw down <span class="key-term">gishmai brachah</span> [rains of blessing] — which are the totality of all the influxes that are drawn to holiness, to the upright of Yisroel who cling to the true <span class="key-term">Tzadikim</span> who are engaged in these rectifications. For on Sukkos, rain is not a sign of blessing — so as not to draw the abundance to the nations who still have some grip, as above. But on Sh'mini Atzeres, when <span class="aramaic">"no stranger shall mingle in their joy"</span> <span class="source">(Sukkah 55b)</span>, then we draw down <span class="key-term">gishmai brachah</span>. This is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Birkas Geshem</span> on Sh'mini Atzeres — for then all their sustenance is nullified, because the <span class="key-term">ahavah sheba'da'as</span> shines with great radiance. And therefore then the abundance is drawn — the aspect of <span class="key-term">gishmai brachah</span> — to the upright of Yisroel alone.</p>
<p>And therefore then we bless for rain and pray that it should be for blessing and not for curse etc. — that the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> should not return and reawaken, <em>chas v'sholom</em>, to draw sustenance from them. And each person, according to his closeness to the true <span class="key-term">Tzadikim</span>, and according to his rectification on Rosh Hashanah, Yom HaKippurim, and Sukkos — so does he merit to draw the abundance as a blessing, to the upright of Yisroel, through the power of the true <span class="key-term">Tzadikim</span> who engage in all these rectifications in completeness.</p>
<div class="os-header">Os 5</div>
<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="scripture">"and he went on his journeys"</span> <span class="source">(Genesis 13:3)</span>, stated regarding Avraham. And our Sages, of blessed memory, expounded <span class="source">(Bereishis Rabbah 41:3)</span>: on his return he repaid his debts [<span class="key-term">hakafos</span>].</p>
<p>For it has already been explained that one must distance oneself from credit purchases and loans — for all the loans are drawn from the overpowering of the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> etc., as above. But nevertheless, sometimes even the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> is compelled to come to loans. And this is for the benefit of the world, in the aspect of <span class="key-term">y'ridah tachlis ha'aliyah</span> [a descent that is the ultimate purpose of ascent]. For through the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> overpowering so greatly that even the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> cannot draw the abundance immediately and is compelled to borrow — through this, the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> subsequently overcomes to an even greater degree, and searches and seeks and finds a great deal of good within the very intensity of the overpowering of the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> that overpowered so greatly.</p>
<p>For this is the aspect of the battle of each Jew — especially of the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> — who does not fall in his spirits and does not despair through all the evil days and difficult times that pass over him. Rather, on the contrary: the more the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> overpower, more and more, he overcomes even more to search and seek even within them, until he finds within them too the letters of the Torah and builds Torah from them, in the aforementioned aspect. For in truth, there is no place so low and degraded in the world, and no day or time or moment in the world, in which words of Torah are not present in a hidden and constricted way — which is the aspect of His G-dliness that enlivens that place and time.</p>
<p>And therefore this man who has already fallen into loans through the overpowering of the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> — his rectification is that he should exert himself with all his might to repay and settle the loans. And through repaying, he fulfills <span class="aramaic">"the repayment of a creditor is a mitzvah"</span>. For he rectifies greatly through this — through having overcome after the loan and having found <span class="key-term">y'mai tov</span> within the very intensity of the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span>, until he drew blessing and abundance to settle the debt. And then, if he is a man of valor, he can afterward draw even greater abundance — for the more one finds good within the very intensity of the greater overpowering of the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span>, the more he makes and builds Torah in the aforementioned aspect and draws greater abundance and blessing, as above.</p>
<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="scripture">"the wicked one borrows and does not repay"</span>, which we mentioned above. "A wicked borrower who does not repay" — these are the aspect of two blemishes. First, he was a "wicked borrower" — for he did not exert himself to seek and find the good within the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span>, until he came to loans, as above. And afterward he added even greater wickedness — "and does not repay" — for he does not exert himself to seek and search now even more, as above. And through this he does not repay. From this comes the fact that usually they become great debtors who are called "ba'alai y'shivos" — <span class="aramaic">"on zetzirsˮ</span> [those who sit, i.e., who are stuck in debt].</p>
<p>But: <span class="scripture">"and the Tzadik is gracious and gives"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 37:21)</span>. That is, the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> is the opposite: not only is he not a debtor, but he also exerts himself each time to find the good within the evil, until he draws blessing and goodly abundance always — until he is gracious and gives and bestows to all through this. And even when [the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span>] comes to loans at times, he overcomes through this very thing to search even more, until he finds even more good and draws and bestows even greater abundance, as above.</p>
<p>And this too is hinted at in this verse — <span class="scripture">"v'Tzadik chonain v'no'sain"</span> — that is, the word <span class="scripture">"v'Tzadik"</span> may also be understood as connected upward to the word <span class="scripture">"loveh"</span> etc. As if to say: "v'Tzadik" — that is, when the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> is compelled to be a borrower, not only does he repay on his date and on time, certainly — but also he is gracious and gives. For through having come to a loan — which stems from the overpowering of the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> until it is difficult to find the good there — through this very thing he overcame with even greater determination, until he found there specifically a great deal of good, until he drew great blessing and abundance — through which he is gracious and gives, as above.</p>
<p>And therefore Avraham Avinu, <em>alav hashalom</em>, who was the first to reveal His G-dliness in the world — whose entire service was in this aspect, in the aspect of: <span class="scripture">"If a person says to you, 'Where is your G-d?' say to him, 'In the great city of Rome'"</span> etc. For then the entire world was far from Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, and all the places where [Avraham] grew up were full of idols. And he overcame his <span class="key-term">yetzer</span> in the aspect of <span class="scripture">"those who do His word, to hearken to the voice of His word"</span> etc. — until he began to build the Torah through this. For the beginning of the building of the Torah was from Avraham, as is known — that is, through the aforementioned aspect.</p>
<p>Therefore it is stated regarding Avraham: <span class="scripture">"and he went on his journeys"</span> — on his return he repaid his debts. For Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> tested him until he was compelled to descend from Eretz Yisroel to Mitzrayim — which was full of immorality and idols in the extreme — and he overcame there until he found there too the good, until he went up from there <span class="scripture">"very heavy in cattle, in silver, and in gold"</span> <span class="source">(Genesis 13:2)</span> etc. And through this: <span class="scripture">"and he went on his journeys"</span> — that he repaid his debts. For this is the essential rectification of the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span>, in the aspect of <span class="scripture">"v'Tzadik chonain v'no'sain"</span>, as above.</p>
<p>And this is also the aspect of <span class="scripture">"and he went on his journeys"</span> — that he lodged at the same inn [as before]. This is the aspect of <span class="scripture">"set your heart toward the highway, the path by which you went"</span> <span class="source">(Jeremiah 31:20)</span>. For the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> and the upright man, when he descends to such degraded places — when he overcomes to search even there for His G-dliness, which is the letters of the Torah — afterward he must return on the path by which he descended there, in order to elevate and refine all the sparks that fell there at the time of the descent, to return everything to <span class="scripture">"the place where his tent had been at the start"</span> <span class="source">(Genesis 13:3)</span> etc.</p>
<div class="os-header">Os 6</div>
<p>And this is [the meaning of] what is adjacent [in the Psalm]: <span class="scripture">"The wicked one borrows"</span> etc., <span class="scripture">"and the Tzadik"</span> etc. — <span class="scripture">"for those blessed by Him shall inherit the earth, and those cursed by Him shall be cut off"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 37:22)</span>. "For those blessed by Him" — who are the <span class="key-term">Tzadikim</span> and the upright — "shall inherit the earth," through the fact that they always overcome to search for the good within the very intensity of the <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span>, as above. For through this Avraham merited to inherit the Land — in the aspect of <span class="scripture">"Avraham was one, and he inherited the Land"</span> <span class="source">(Ezekiel 33:24)</span>.</p>
<p>And this is: <span class="scripture">"By Hashem are the steps of a man established"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Psalms 37:23)</span> — which is adjacent to this. "For by Hashem are the steps of a man established" — that He sometimes causes a person to fall to such low places as a test, whether he will overcome there to search and seek the good, as is fitting, within such an evil place and time, as above. And this is: <span class="scripture">"and He desires his way"</span> — for Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> desires to see the way of the person: whether he will overcome to choose the path of good, to search and seek until he finds there too the goodness of Hashem — until he returns to his place with even greater elevation.</p>
<p>And this is: <span class="scripture">"Though he fall, he shall not be cast down, for Hashem supports his hand"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Psalms 37:24)</span>. This is the aspect of the rectification regarding all of the above, in the aspect of <span class="key-term">y'ridah tachlis ha'aliyah</span> — the aspect of <span class="aramaic">"Yisroel were not exiled among the nations except so that converts should be added to them"</span> <span class="source">(Pesachim 87b)</span> etc. And as it is written: <span class="scripture">"and there shall remain even he, for our G-d"</span> <span class="source">(Zechariah 9:7)</span> etc. And as it is written: <span class="scripture">"For seven times does the Tzadik fall and rise"</span> <span class="source">(Proverbs 24:16)</span> etc. For He will never allow the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span> to be shaken — for Hashem supports his hand through all the falls and descents in the world.</p>
<p>And through this: <span class="scripture">"I was young and also I have aged, and I have not seen a Tzadik forsaken"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Psalms 37:25)</span>. Rather, on the contrary: <span class="scripture">"all the day he is gracious and lends, and his seed is a blessing"</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 37:26)</span>. For [the <span class="key-term">Tzadik</span>] merits afterward to be a lender and not a borrower, as it is written: <span class="scripture">"And you shall lend to many nations, and you shall not borrow"</span> etc. <span class="source">(Deuteronomy 28:12)</span>. And all of this is through the aforementioned aspect — through having overcome each time, through all the falls and descents in the world, through all the times and <span class="key-term">y'mai ra</span> that overpowered and spread as they spread: to seek and search even there, each time more and more, until he found even there the good — which are the letters of the Torah, which is His G-dliness that enlivens them. And through this he rectified very greatly and made a paved road for all the world to return to Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span> through this. For through the Torah that he gathered and refined and built from such places, and added upon them wondrous light from above, as above — through this he illuminated for the entire world this path: that each person should return, even the lowest of the low, to Hashem <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>, from every place where he is, as above. And through this he draws goodly abundance and blessing to all who are joined to him — in the aspect of <span class="scripture">"all the day he is gracious and lends, and his seed is a blessing,"</span> as above.</p>

<div class="translator-summary">
<h3>Summary of Hilchos Halva'ah 5</h3>
<p>This halachah (6 osiyos, based on LM I:54 "Mi HaIsh HeChafetz Chaim") locates the root of all debt in the dichotomy of y'mai tov / y'mai ra. Borrowing = believing the present is purely "bad days" and the future will be "good days" — a split that is itself the blemish. The person who believes that even within the y'mai ra themselves good is hidden (the letters of Torah clothed in all languages, all contexts, all hardships) will find parnasah davar yom b'yomo and never need to borrow.</p>
<p>The Yiddish aside in Os 1 — "di tzayt iz shlecht," everyone always says the times are bad, generation after generation — is a striking moment of self-aware, almost wry commentary. The point: if every generation says "now is bad, later will be good," the schema itself is the problem.</p>
<p>The sh'tar = writing = zikaron. Shich'chah (forgetfulness) is a function of time; writing overcomes time by making the good prevail over the y'mai ra. Settling debts = making good prevail = a mitzvah ("pri'as ba'al chov mitzvah"). Os 2 traces the servitude of debt back through "arur Canaan" → "arurah ha'adamah" → chet Adam HaRishon: Adam failed to subdue the y'mai ra on that very day → "evil bayom yivadah ka'aso" / "ki b'yom achalcha" — "bayom" specifically.</p>
<p>Os 3-4 map the entire Yamim Nora'im cycle through LM I:54's framework: RH/YK = t'shuvah through finding good within evil → kol shofar → 5 inuyim = 5 kolos → build Torah → approach shalom (= Sukkah) → Sukkos = ahavah sheba'yamim, still has grip for 70 nations → na'anu'im illuminate ahavah sheba'da'as into yamim → Arvai Nachal = finding good in posh'ai Yisroel → Hoshana Rabbah: beat willows to reveal Torah within y'mai ra → Sh'mini Atzeres: ahavah sheba'da'as fully radiates, no more grip for nations → Birkas Geshem drawn only for Yisroel → Simchas Torah: join Torah's end (ahavah sheba'yamim) to its beginning (Bereishis = reishis hamachashavah = ahavah sheba'da'as).</p>
<p>Os 5 is the crown: Avraham descending to Mitzrayim = y'ridah tachlis ha'aliyah. Even the Tzadik sometimes must borrow (= y'mai ra overpower) — but this descent enables finding deeper good than ever. "Vayeilech l'masa'av — b'chaziraso para hakafosav": on his return he repaid his debts, ascending "heavy in cattle, silver, and gold." The "loveh rasha v'lo y'shalaim" = two blemishes (borrows wickedly + fails to repay). But "v'Tzadik chonain v'no'sain" = even when compelled to borrow, the Tzadik finds so much good that he not only repays but becomes a giver. "Sisi libech lamesila derech halachti" — return by the same road to elevate what fell during the descent.</p>
<p class="translator-note">Based on Likutay Moharan I:54 "Mi HaIsh HeChafetz Chaim"</p>
</div>

